# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
react | definition | React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces out of reusable components, and the idea that made it popular is one-way data flow: a component receives data through props, and the only way that data changes is through a state hook that re-renders it. That constraint is what makes React apps predictable, because there is a single owner of every piece of data and no two parts of the tree quietly disagree about it. The rendering model is a virtual document object model, a plain JavaScript description of what the interface should look like, which React compares against the previous one and updates only the real nodes that changed. The downside is that the extra layer and the build tooling add real complexity to a small project, and the first few hours are often spent on tooling rather than on the feature. | virtual dom, component, one way data flow, state, library, build step, hooks | what is react, is react a framework or library, should i learn react | React | React
jsx | definition | JSX is a syntax extension that lets you write what looks like HTML inside JavaScript, and it compiles to a function call that creates an element object rather than producing a string of markup. A tag becomes a function invocation, so {name} inside it substitutes a value, and a component that starts with a capital letter is treated as a custom component and called rather than emitted as a literal tag. The compiler is Babel or an equivalent tool built into most React toolchains, and the compiled output is plain JavaScript that a browser can run, which is what makes JSX an extension rather than a new language. The point worth knowing is that JSX creates a tree of plain objects, not a DOM node, so the fast comparison React performs is a comparison of JavaScript objects. Browser tools can display it and JavaScript files can contain it, which means a stray typo surfaces as a syntax error at compile time rather than as a blank page. | babel, transpiler, syntax extension, element, expression, curly braces, compiler | what is jsx, is jsx html, do i need babel for jsx | jsx | jsx
react hook | definition | A hook is a function beginning with use that lets a function component use state and other React features, and the naming rule is not decoration: React identifies hooks by that prefix and relies on the calls happening in the same order on every render. useState returns a value and a setter, useEffect runs code after rendering for things like fetching data or subscribing to an event, and useMemo caches an expensive calculation between renders. The rule against calling a hook conditionally or inside a loop follows from how the runtime tracks them, since it matches them up by position, so skipping one on some path makes every hook after it read the wrong state. Hooks also solved the problem of sharing behaviour between components, which the older class component approach handled with inheritance and higher order components, both of which made code hard to follow. The criticism that arrived later is that hooks plus dependency arrays are easy to get subtly wrong, and forgetting a dependency is a common source of stale data. | use state, use effect, dependency array, function component, closure, re-render, memoization | what is a react hook, why does a hook start with use, what is the dependency array | react hook | react hook
props | definition | In React a prop is a value a parent passes down to a child, and it is the only way information flows downward. Props are read-only for the child: a component that wants to change what it was given does not write to the prop, it calls a state setter, and the new value travels back up and then down again as a new prop. Children are passed the same way, as props.children when a component wraps content, and functions passed down as props are how a parent hands a child the ability to call back. The design rule is that props are immutable data describing what something is, while state is the part that changes over time, and mixing the two is what causes a component that re-renders when nothing visible changed. Because props flow one way and never upward, sharing state between distant components usually means lifting it to their closest common parent or using a context, which is the main structural decision a React application makes. | properties, read only, children, state lifting, one way, immutable, context | what are props in react, can you change props, what is state lifting | props | props
